Six shared failures have explicit runtime causes:
- find: USER is absent, so its -user argument becomes -exec.
- hostname set and rm's read-only tmpfs: EPERM without CAP_SYS_ADMIN.
- three losetup cases: the backing path exceeds loop_info64.lo_file_name's
  64-byte field; the original xstrncpy reports the overflow, leaving later
  enumeration/detach assertions without the expected filename.

The small C prerequisite probe returns 77 (unavailable, not PASS) with unset
USER and the original container capabilities in all four fixed reference
builds. With USER=root and CAP_SYS_ADMIN, all four reference builds and four
crossed EvoCC O0/O2 builds return zero. It sets the hostname to its existing
value, mounts one read-only 4 KiB tmpfs, verifies missing-file stat/ENOENT,
unmounts and removes its temporary directory. No host mount is touched.
An initial fixture mistakenly expected unlink of a missing read-only file to
return ENOENT; it returned EROFS. The corrected stat prerequisite and its new
GREEN log supersede that failed diagnostic, not a compiler implementation.

Shared adapter: USER=$(id -un), short /tmp/tb.XXXXXX runtime directory, and
CAP_SYS_ADMIN only in this disposable container, alongside the already-needed
CAP_LINUX_IMMUTABLE. No target object/source is copied into the runtime tree.
Binary/object audits and the unmodified upstream assertions remain mandatory.
261 Python ladder regressions and Ruff PASS; the new real-shell test verifies
both an inherited wrong USER and the complete backing-file path length.

Unchanged five-collection replay (find, hostname, losetup, rm, tar) selects only
runtime test executable bits, not source contents: BOTH sides complete
155 PASS / 1 FAIL / 1 SKIP. The only FAIL is tar ownership. An earlier attempt
to pass collection names as test.sh arguments invoked upstream single.sh and
could not build from the deliberately source-free staging tree; that setup
ERROR with zero outcomes is not counted as test evidence.

Remaining tar ownership is a source-bound contradictory expected hash:
tests/tar.test SHA256
6ac2f1515ff076211d30d4f1ab3012a3acd74271a5849ab77f10706599bb65e1.
toys/posix/tar.c SHA256
99d258976fc742c2b5fb595e7bb94b7c7f5cee19a9433ae670dd5261d2cad41f.
The test's TAR command fixes --owner root --group sys --mtime @1234567890.
Its first empty dir/file archive expects d9e7fb3884430d29e7eed0dc04a2593dd260df14;
after chown nobody:nogroup, the identical overriding command instead expects
2d7b96c7025987215f5a41f10eaa84311160afdb. Frozen tar.c overrides st_uid/st_gid
before serializing the header. The small shell reduction invokes both audited
binaries before/after the chown: cmp of COMPLETE archives succeeds, and the
original 1536-byte digest is d9e7fb3884430d29e7eed0dc04a2593dd260df14 on both.
No expected hash, assertion or test is edited. The raw original FAIL remains
FAIL; this evidence classifies it, not converts it to PASS.

